DS - Something important has come up regarding percentage calculations and the digits after the . meaning 1.1111111111111111 to infinity when percentage is calculated in a transaction.
We only see the 2 digits after the decimal - What happens to the rest of the digits, please?
This is a CRITICAL issue in financial systems! Let me explain what happens to those "invisible" digits and provide the proper solution.

The Problem: Floating Point Precision Errors
When you calculate percentages, you're dealing with repeating decimals that computers can't store exactly in binary.

Example Calculations:
javascript
// These create infinite repeating decimals in binary:
1 / 3 = 0.3333333333333333...
1 / 6 = 0.1666666666666666...
5 / 6 = 0.8333333333333333...

// Even "simple" percentages:
12.5% = 0.1250000000000000... (finite)
33.33% = 0.3333000000000000... (finite but with precision loss)
What Happens to the "Lost" Digits?
1. Rounding Errors Accumulate
javascript
// Example: 1/3 calculation over multiple transactions
let total = 0;
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  total += 1/3; // 0.3333333333333333 each time
}
console.log(total); // 0.9999999999999999 instead of 1.0
2. Financial Discrepancies
typescript
// Bad approach - using floating point
const calculateCommission = (amount: number, percentage: number) => {
  return amount * (percentage / 100); // PRECISION LOSS!
};

const amount = 100.00;
const commission = calculateCommission(amount, 33.33);
console.log(commission); // 33.329999999999998 instead of 33.33
The Professional Solution: Integer-Based Arithmetic
1. Enhanced Database Schema with Precision Handling
